/** The rule: kits propose, the request chooses, the game embeds one coherent set. */
export const GAMEPLAY_CHOICE_RULE = "Kits offer capabilities that can be switched off and tuned; the game makes one high-level gameplay choice and enables only the coherent set that choice needs: what is not chosen is off and invisible, and no kit default is imposed on the game. The generic modules propose choices, the request picks one: name the kind of game, take its control scheme from the table below (usually the only one allowed), and embed only what that scheme uses on each device — its input sources, its on-screen controls on a touch screen, its settings (remapping, sensitivity, inversion, capture, dead zone only where the scheme uses them). Every option is a choice of the request too: the network input frame is embedded only for a multiplayer game, each other option only when asked. Write the choice into the project: gripforge_game_project create with controls=<scheme>, or action=controls scheme=<scheme> on an existing game (dry_run first, reset=true to drop old key overrides). A dungeon crawler is played with the mouse, not on W A S D. In play, input.scheme (a default kit) implements that choice: it follows the device in hand (keyboard and mouse, gamepad, touch) with the variant of the scheme for it, takes the vehicle scheme while the player drives, gives the input to the interface while a menu, a dialogue or a text field is open, and the Options show per device only the controls the scheme uses; tune it within the choice (input.scheme allowed: the closed list the player may switch between, usually one; variants; composites for held / double / charged / chorded / combo actions), never by enabling every option.";

/** One line per kind of game: its control scheme and what it embeds on each device. */
export const GAMEPLAY_CHOICES_TEXT = "- MOBA → `moba_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: tap_world, settings: touch_layout). A hero is ordered with the mouse (right click to move / attack, abilities toward the cursor); a gamepad moves it with the stick. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style bold, on: highlight, cursor, confirm, fail, sound, orderMarker; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- Real-time strategy → `rts` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: click to move, settings: remap, deadzone; touch: tap to move, on screen: stick + buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). Units are selected and ordered with the cursor; nothing is moved with W A S D. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, cursor, confirm, fail, sound, moveMarker, orderMarker; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- Dungeon crawler / action RPG seen from above → `arpg_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). Played with the mouse: click to walk, click an enemy to attack, right click for the special; the left stick on a gamepad, a tap on a touch screen. W A S D is not the default for this genre. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, nearbyHint, worldPrompt, cursor, holdGauge, confirm, fail, sound, orderMarker; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- Top-down shooter / twin-stick roguelike → `top_down` (mouse+keys: WASD, right click = aim, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: virtual stick, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout). Constant dodging needs direct movement (W A S D / left stick) with the cursor or right stick to aim. The game may also allow: arpg_click. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, worldPrompt, holdGauge, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Point-and-click adventure → `point_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). A free cursor: click where to go and what to use. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, nearbyHint, worldPrompt, cursor, confirm, fail, sound, moveMarker; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- First-person shooter / survival → `fps` (mouse+keys: WASD, captured mouse, right click = aim, settings: remap, mouse_sensitivity, invert_y, pointer_capture; gamepad: stick, right stick looks, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, stick_sensitivity, invert_y, aim_assist; touch: virtual stick, right stick looks, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout, stick_sensitivity, invert_y). W A S D and the captured mouse to look (right stick on a gamepad). Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style subtle, on: highlight, worldPrompt, holdGauge, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Third-person action / open world → `third_person` (mouse+keys: WASD, captured mouse, right click = aim, settings: remap, mouse_sensitivity, invert_y, pointer_capture; gamepad: stick, right stick looks, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, stick_sensitivity, invert_y, aim_assist; touch: virtual stick, right stick looks, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout, stick_sensitivity, invert_y). W A S D relative to the camera and the captured mouse (right stick) to turn it. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, nearbyHint, worldPrompt, holdGauge, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Platformer → `platformer` (mouse+keys: WASD, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, settings: remap, deadzone; touch: virtual stick, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout). Direct movement and jump, no pointer. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style subtle, on: worldPrompt, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Fighting → `fighting` (mouse+keys: WASD, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, settings: remap, deadzone; touch: virtual stick, on screen: buttons, settings: touch_layout). One key per blow, no pointer. No interaction.feedback.\n- Racing / vehicles → `vehicle` (mouse+keys: vehicle, settings: remap; gamepad: vehicle, settings: remap, deadzone; touch: vehicle, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout). Throttle, brake and steering; no pointer. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style subtle, on: worldPrompt, confirm, fail.\n- Turn-based combat / tactics → `point_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). Each turn is played with the cursor or a tap (combat.turns, tactics.grid): pick a unit or an action, then its cell or its target; no direct movement. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, cursor, confirm, fail, sound; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- Card game / deckbuilder / pack opening → `point_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). Cards and packs are played with the cursor or a tap (cards.deck on combat.turns; collection.packs for a pack-opening or gacha game with an album): pick a card or a pack, then its target or the next object to turn over; no direct movement. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, cursor, confirm, fail, sound; interaction.system pointerTarget: true.\n- Puzzle → `third_person` (mouse+keys: WASD, captured mouse, right click = aim, settings: remap, mouse_sensitivity, invert_y, pointer_capture; gamepad: stick, right stick looks, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, stick_sensitivity, invert_y, aim_assist; touch: virtual stick, right stick looks, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout, stick_sensitivity, invert_y). The puzzle kits (puzzle.circuits, puzzle.beams) sit in the world: the player reaches them with the game's character, seen from behind by default, in first person or with the cursor when the game is built that way. The game may also allow: fps, point_click. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style default, on: highlight, nearbyHint, worldPrompt, holdGauge, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Sports / ball game → `third_person` (mouse+keys: WASD, captured mouse, right click = aim, settings: remap, mouse_sensitivity, invert_y, pointer_capture; gamepad: stick, right stick looks, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, stick_sensitivity, invert_y, aim_assist; touch: virtual stick, right stick looks, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout, stick_sensitivity, invert_y). sport.ball on foot: the athlete moves with W A S D or the left stick and kicks where it faces; in its car mode, the vehicle controls. The game may also allow: vehicle. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style subtle, on: worldPrompt, confirm, fail, sound.\n- Golf / mini-golf → `point_click` (mouse+keys: click to move, right click = context, settings: remap; gamepad: stick, aim assist, settings: remap, deadzone, aim_assist; touch: tap to move, on screen: buttons + tap_world, settings: touch_layout). sport.golf: the shot is aimed with the free cursor (the ball goes where the pointer is) and played with a click or a tap that stops the power gauge; nobody walks, so no W A S D and no captured pointer; the left stick turns the aim on a gamepad. No interaction.feedback.\n- Flight → `vehicle` (mouse+keys: vehicle, settings: remap; gamepad: vehicle, settings: remap, deadzone; touch: vehicle, on screen: stick + buttons, settings: touch_layout). Throttle and steering on the keys or the sticks, no pointer; the catalogue has no dedicated flight kit yet, so the vehicle controls drive it. Interaction feedback: interaction.feedback style subtle, on: worldPrompt, confirm, fail.";

/** How the interaction feedback follows the device. */
export const GAMEPLAY_FEEDBACK_DEVICES = "Per device: the cursor per kind of target only with a mouse; on a gamepad the prompt shows the button of the active gamepad, on a touch screen the text alone (ui.prompts / input.actions promptFor); the designated target comes from input.scheme target() (cursor, aim axis, stick).";

/** The interaction rule (interaction.feedback, interaction.use). */
export const GAMEPLAY_INTERACTION_RULE = "Interactions must be visible. Install interaction.feedback for any game with interactive objects (not for a pure fighting game) and set its elements from the gameplay table row; never draw an interaction prompt in a kit or a script: kits that offer an interaction pass their kind to interaction.use.register(entity, label, action, kind) (or interactable.kind on an archetype), and slow uses get a hold (interactable.hold / interaction.system.holdSeconds) instead of a custom timer. Styles and kinds are data (feedback_styles, interaction_kinds): restyle with them, do not recode. Leave the player settings (interaction.feedback.settings) to the menu.";

